# eu-u9xj.6 (PP) fixture for the FORK half of
# test_pp_gc_collects_during_par_map in harness_test.rs.
#
# The driver has to hold heap handles across `force()` calls: mapping N
# elements means accumulating N results while forcing each one. A handle on
# the Rust stack is invisible to the collector, so if the driver kept them
# there, a collection during the map would move the objects out from under it
# and leave it dereferencing freed or relocated memory. They live in the
# machine's root set instead (`IntrinsicMachine::gc_root_push`).
#
# `EU_GC_VERIFY=2` cannot see that class of bug — it verifies reachability
# *from roots*, and the whole point is that these handles were not roots. What
# catches it is running the map with collection actually happening during it,
# under `EU_GC_STRESS=1` so every collection evacuates rather than merely
# marking, and comparing the answer with the `map` oracle beside this file.
#
# Much more work than pp_gc_stress_fixture.eu, because the fork path splits
# it W ways and each worker must allocate enough *within its own chunk* to
# trigger a collection, or no collection happens in the worker and the gate
# does not gate.
#
# Collection is triggered by allocation volume since the last collection,
# capped at the headroom below the heap limit (eu-oaz72.4), so under the
# `--heap-limit-mib 8` the test passes a worker collects once it has
# allocated a few MiB: each element allocates a 1000-element intermediate
# list. Before eu-oaz72.4 the heap collected only at its limit, and a 100-
# element list sufficed because a forked worker inherited a heap already at
# the limit; under the volume trigger that shape no longer gates. Verified by
# fault injection at W=4 with --heap-limit-mib 8: reading `f` and the
# elements onto the Rust stack once, before the worker loop, is detected
# (exit 1, 2/2) with the 1000-element shape and NOT with the 100-element
# one. If this fixture is ever retuned, re-verify by fault injection that it
# still fails when the root set is removed.

f(x): range(0, 1000) map((_0 + x)) sum
xs: range(1, 60)
r: xs par-map(f)
n: r sum
